  "reasoning": "The patient presents with fever, headache, swollen lymph nodes, weakness, and fatigue. These are hallmark systemic symptoms of a viral upper respiratory infection. We can systematically rule out other options: absence of rash eliminates chicken pox, dengue, impetigo, psoriasis, fungal infection, and drug reaction; lack of breathing difficulties eliminates bronchial asthma and pneumonia; no gastrointestinal complaints eliminate typhoid, peptic ulcer disease, and GERD; absence of urinary or metabolic changes eliminates UTI and diabetes; no focal neurological or vascular signs eliminate migraine, hypertension, and cervical spondylosis; lack of cyclical chills/heavy sweating eliminates malaria; and no yellowing of skin/eyes eliminates jaundice. The combination of low-grade fever, headache, reactive lymphadenopathy, and generalized malaise/fatigue is most consistent with the common cold.",
